  architecture guard: the DIAS runtime has no policy engine
    ✔ the deployed contracts never load the retained rule engine or disagreement guard
    ✔ the chaincode package excludes every retained policy-engine module
    ✔ runtime modules stay inside the package and never reach the offline oracle or guard
    ✔ loads the DIAS modules for verified requests, authorization, and lifecycle
    ✔ never loads an LLM recommendation, provenance, or model-registration module

  rank ladder
    ✔ orders each department from low to high
    ✔ gives every role in every department a rank
    ✔ never compares ranks across departments

  authority resolution
    ✔ makes the highest-ranked officer at a station its head
    ✔ makes the highest-ranked officer in a district its head
    ✔ keeps departments separate
    ✔ routes a request to the station head when that head has the clearance
    ✔ routes past a station head who lacks the clearance, up to the district head
    ✔ skips the station tier for a requester with no station
    ✔ never routes a request to the requester themselves
    ✔ returns nothing when no authority in reach holds the clearance

  canonical ledger state
    the serializer is independent of insertion order
      ✔ produces identical bytes for the same content inserted in any order
      ✔ survives the spread that caused the divergence
      ✔ canonicalises nested objects and preserves array order
      ✔ still distinguishes different content
      ✔ refuses anything that is not a JSON object
      ✔ writes the canonical bytes through putJson
    no contract may bypass the serializer
      ✔ accessContract.js writes JSON state only through putJson
      ✔ accessContract.js never serialises STATE with JSON.stringify
      ✔ accessContract.js builds no event payload from spread state
      ✔ auditContract.js writes JSON state only through putJson
      ✔ auditContract.js never serialises STATE with JSON.stringify
      ✔ auditContract.js builds no event payload from spread state
      ✔ governanceContract.js writes JSON state only through putJson
      ✔ governanceContract.js never serialises STATE with JSON.stringify
      ✔ governanceContract.js builds no event payload from spread state
      ✔ recordContract.js writes JSON state only through putJson
      ✔ recordContract.js never serialises STATE with JSON.stringify
      ✔ recordContract.js builds no event payload from spread state
      ✔ userContract.js writes JSON state only through putJson
      ✔ userContract.js never serialises STATE with JSON.stringify
      ✔ userContract.js builds no event payload from spread state
      ✔ every contract imports the shared writer

  DIAS access workflow safeguards
    request submission
      ✔ commits the structured action and purpose and keeps identity out of the verified request
      ✔ rejects invalid request fields
      ✔ binds the request to the certificate-matched UserProfile
    auditor decision stage
      ✔ allows only AuditMSP district heads, with a valid decision and LLM agreement
      ✔ prevents a requester from deciding their own request
      ✔ rejects a decision when governed facts changed after the request
      ✔ bounds revocation reasons and identifiers
    read access
      ✔ limits a request to its requester and district heads
      ✔ shows district heads the pending queue with the committed request only
      ✔ lets district heads query authorizations and their history
      ✔ limits access decisions to their subject and district heads

  DIAS access workflow
    request log
      ✔ records who asked for which record and waits for the auditor
      ✔ writes the LLM recommendation value but none of its reasoning or provenance
    decision log: auditor FORCE_ALLOW that agreed with an LLM ALLOW
      ✔ grants access, records AGREED, and creates no dynamic authorization
    decision log: auditor FORCE_DENY that agreed with an LLM DENY
      ✔ denies access without creating a dynamic authorization
    decision log: auditor FORCE_ALLOW that did not agree with an LLM DENY
      ✔ grants access and creates an exact-record dynamic authorization
    decision log: auditor FORCE_DENY that did not agree with an LLM ALLOW
      ✔ denies access, records NOT_AGREED, and creates no dynamic authorization
    decision log: no LLM recommendation
      ✔ lets the auditor decide and never creates a dynamic authorization
    public decision log
      ✔ lets any signed-in member read who asked for what and what was decided
      ✔ shows an automatic grant as decided by its dynamic authorization
      ✔ returns the newest entries first and refuses an out-of-range limit
    reuse of an active dynamic authorization
      ✔ grants a repeat request with the auditor recorded as skipped
    no broad reuse
      ✔ does not reuse the authorization for another record, action, purpose, or user
      ✔ does not reuse the authorization after a governed fact changes
    explicit revocation
      ✔ requires a district head and a reason, records the change, and stops reuse
      ✔ creates a new generation when a revoked scope is approved again
    expiry
      ✔ stops reuse after validUntilUtc and records the expiry transition
      ✔ rejects an expiry when no dynamic authorization is created
    FORCE_DENY
      ✔ never revokes other authorizations

  AuditContract
    GetRequestAuditTrail
      ✔ reconstructs a reviewed request that created a dynamic authorization
      ✔ reconstructs a reused authorization with the auditor skipped, and a later revocation
      ✔ lets the requester read their own trail and refuses other identities
    record-level trail and verification
      ✔ lists the DIAS requests and release grants for a record
      ✔ confirms a matching payload hash and flags a tampered one
    direct-ledger access events
      ✔ derives the actor from the signing identity and stores the event
      ✔ allows reviewers to query and rejects ordinary departments
      ✔ rejects malformed event fields and limits

  DIAS dynamic authorization scope
    ✔ is exact: user, record, case, action, and purpose each change the scope

  DIAS dynamic authorization creation
    ✔ records the originating request, auditor decision, LLM agreement, scope, and conditions
    ✔ is refused unless the auditor FORCE_ALLOW did not agree with an LLM DENY
    ✔ validates identifiers, auditor identity, and expiry
    ✔ increments the generation when it replaces an earlier authorization

  DIAS dynamic authorization matching
    ✔ matches only an active, unexpired authorization with unchanged conditions
    ✔ does not match when no authorization or a different scope is stored
    ✔ reports revoked, superseded, expired, and changed-condition misses

  DIAS dynamic authorization state transitions
    ✔ revokes without mutating the previous state
    ✔ expires and supersedes with an incremented state version

  DIAS lifecycle events
    ✔ writes ordered, transaction-bound events under the request ID
    ✔ keeps request event streams isolated even when IDs share a prefix
    ✔ stores authorization events in their own key space
    ✔ emits one chaincode event naming every committed stage

  DIAS verified request
    ✔ builds the documented shape with booleans and nulls normalized
    ✔ rejects missing groups, extra fields, and wrong types
    ✔ hashes independently of key order

  DIAS recommendation schema
    ✔ accepts a valid response and normalizes key order
    ✔ allows only binary recommendations with matching reason codes
    ✔ validates lists against the registered policy vocabulary
    ✔ separates generation failures from recommendations

  GovernanceContract
    ✔ creates and queries a department
    ✔ blocks anyone outside the district authority
    ✔ creates, reads and lists a governed case
    ✔ validates case ownership and protected classifications
    ✔ lets a supervisor assign users without duplicates
    ✔ rejects unauthorized case creation and assignment
    ✔ records prosecution/court workflow metadata with valid transitions
    ✔ rejects invalid court workflow transitions
    who may create a department
      ✔ accepts a district head of the authority organisation
      ✔ refuses a working officer of that department
      ✔ refuses a station head, who is not a district authority

  canonical request facts drive the deterministic safety check
    TEST 1 - the model read the request the way it was committed
      ✔ keeps the model result when action, purpose, decision and reason all agree
    TEST 2 - the model read a different operation than the one invoked
      ✔ escalates when the canonical action is view and the model says export
      ✔ evaluates policy on the canonical action, not the one the model reported
      ✔ escalates when the model reports a different purpose than the one committed
    TEST 3 - trusted context implies DENY, the model proposes ALLOW
      ✔ escalates rather than granting
    TEST 4 - trusted context implies ALLOW, the model proposes DENY
      ✔ escalates rather than denying
    TEST 5 - the decision matches but the reason code conflicts
      ✔ escalates an ALLOW carried by a denial reason code
      ✔ escalates a reason code the policy did not reach, even at the same decision
    TEST 6 - purpose is established independently of the model
      ✔ takes the purpose the requester committed, never the model classification
      ✔ never shows the canonical operation or purpose to the model
      ✔ rejects a purpose outside the policy vocabulary before inference
    TEST 7 - the explanation describes the escalation, not the proposal
      ✔ explains a policy disagreement without reusing the proposed ALLOW reason
      ✔ explains an input disagreement in its own terms
      ✔ records the committed request, not the model reading, in parsedRequest
    TEST 8 - disagreement never substitutes the deterministic outcome
      ✔ escalates instead of adopting the policy outcome: model ALLOW against a policy DENY
      ✔ escalates instead of adopting the policy outcome: model DENY against a policy ALLOW
      ✔ escalates instead of adopting the policy outcome: model DENY against a policy DENY for a different reason
      ✔ escalates instead of adopting the policy outcome: model misread the operation
      ✔ exhaustively: no advisory over the whole reason vocabulary ever yields a
      ✔ never lets the model emit a system reason code itself

  DIAS dynamic access policy
    canonical request fingerprint
      ✔ is stable across record and case identifiers when properties and assignment match
      ✔ changes when any governed match field changes
    rule provenance
      ✔ creates a versioned active rule only for DENY to FORCE_ALLOW

  policyEngine (crime-policy-v2)
    ✔ allows a fully compliant request with decisive attributes
    ✔ denies when the credential is not active (rule 1)
    ✔ denies a missing or unknown purpose (rule 2)
    ✔ denies when RBAC gives the role no such permission (rule 3)
    ✔ does not let an AuditMSP title inherit an operational PoliceMSP role
    ✔ escalates a sealed record to the court for everyone outside it (rule 4)
    ✔ lets the court open its own sealed record without escalation (rule 4)
    ✔ denies juvenile records outside the narrow legal exception (rule 5)
    ✔ denies victim-protected raw content to forensics (rule 5b)
    ✔ denies any request that crosses a district, for every role (rule 6)
    ✔ has no emergency override of any kind
    ✔ denies unassigned officers with a counterfactual (rule 7)
    ✔ exempts cross-case roles from assignment but never from jurisdiction
    ✔ denies insufficient clearance rather than escalating (rule 8)
    ✔ treats a missing clearance attribute as no clearance (rule 8)
    ✔ never blocks on the witness flag alone
    ✔ is deterministic: same input, same output

  structured explanation materialization
    ✔ defines explanation details for every active reason code
    ✔ TEST 1 - materializes the correct ALLOW explanation
    ✔ TEST 2 - materializes the correct DENY explanation
    ✔ TEST 3 - materializes the correct normal ESCALATE explanation
    ✔ TEST 4 - explains MODEL_POLICY_DISAGREEMENT from proposed ALLOW
    ✔ TEST 5 - explains MODEL_POLICY_DISAGREEMENT from proposed DENY
    ✔ materializes MODEL_INPUT_DISAGREEMENT from the effective input-conflict reason
    ✔ TEST 6 - rejects a model decision that conflicts with its reason
    ✔ TEST 7 - detects tampered explanation text after the attacker recomputes outputHash
    ✔ TEST 8 - detects tampered decisiveAttributes after the attacker recomputes outputHash
    ✔ TEST 9 - detects a tampered counterfactual after the attacker recomputes outputHash
    ✔ TEST 10 - rejects an unknown reason code
    ✔ exhaustively matches each model reason explanation to the policy evidence
    ✔ gives every system-generated escalation a policy-condition counterfactual

